  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Double-striped Bluet is a small damselfly belonging to the Coenagrionidae family, characterized by its delicate blue and black coloration. It is a common species throughout New England during the summer months, often found near freshwater environments.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Double Black Stripes",
      "description": "Two distinctive black stripes run down the length of the abdomen on a bright blue background, giving the species its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Blue Coloration",
      "description": "Males display brilliant blue on the thorax and abdomen, while females may show blue, green, or tan coloration",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "At 1.2-1.4 inches long, it is among the smallest damselflies in New England",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Double-striped Bluets prefer slow-moving streams, ponds, and marshes with abundant aquatic vegetation where they hunt for small insects near the water's surface.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "These damselflies are known for their mating behavior, where pairs remain connected in a heart-shaped position called a tandem while females lay eggs directly into aquatic plants.",
